          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"balzac-illusions-perdues-fe02b218ca608edb\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":1,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.94,\"analysis\":\"Coralie’s conditional declaration—“Je t’aimerais laid et malade!” (“I would love you [even if you were] ugly and sick”) primarily avows a love presented as independent of Lucien’s beauty and health. Its pledge-like assurance of constancy gives slight performative support, but the wording chiefly represents the depth of her existing emotional state rather than undertaking a distinct obligation. Despite its passionate setting and exclamation mark, there is no positive evidence that the words are a reflex-like affective discharge.\",\"ambiguity\":\"The counterfactual claim of continued love can sound like a relational pledge, but it does not clearly establish an undertaking beyond the avowal itself.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"Coralie directly whispers the conditional love declaration to Lucien as she sits at the table.\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"Coralie has just privately told Florine that she loves Lucien and arranged to remove Camusot as an obstacle. The narrator explicitly characterizes her feeling as pure, sincere first love, intensified by passion and admiration of Lucien’s beauty. Her statement therefore reassures Lucien that her love is not merely attraction to his present beauty. Its polished paradox—loving him even ugly and ill—is described as a striking phrase for a poet and strongly affects him, but those contextual effects do not independently establish expressive/reflexive force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“Je t’aimerais laid et malade!”\",\"supports\":\"The conditional declaration presents Coralie’s love as persisting even under imagined loss of Lucien’s beauty and health, strongly supporting T and weakly suggesting pledge-like constancy.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"Immediately beforehand Coralie tells Florine, “Non, ma chère, je l’aime!”\",\"supports\":\"Her unqualified avowal that she loves Lucien confirms that the highlighted conditional expresses an existing loving state rather than a merely hypothetical possibility.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The narrator describes “L’amour pur, sincère, le premier amour enfin” and says it “donnèrent l’esprit du cœur à Coralie.”\",\"supports\":\"The narration explicitly frames Coralie’s utterance as an ingenious expression of sincere first love, supporting dominant T while offering no specific evidence of reflex-like discharge.\",\"confidence\":0.98},{\"evidence_id\":\"e4\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“dit-elle à l’oreille de Lucien en se mettant à table” and the response “Quel mot pour un poète!”\",\"supports\":\"The intimate delivery and Lucien’s strong reception establish emotional and rhetorical intensity, but not by themselves E or P.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e5\",\"source\":\"supplied_metadata\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The occurrence is tagged as affirmative conditional/modal French (“fr_conditional”).\",\"supports\":\"Confirms the grammatical conditionality underlying the imagined adverse circumstances, without changing the utterance’s principal avowal force.\",\"confidence\":0.99}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the core avowal, with a small P score preserving its pledge-like assurance of constancy. Delivery and narrative intensity remain contextual because nothing important is lost by declining to posit independent E or O force.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":\"The analytical translation was used only as an aid; interpretive claims are grounded in the supplied French context and metadata.\"}"
        }
